        'description' => 'Recurrent unexpected panic attacks, 13 somatic/cognitive symptoms (4-symptom threshold), 1-month anticipatory worry, and the transdiagnostic specifier.',
        'overview' => "Panic Disorder is characterized by recurrent, unexpected panic attacks accompanied by at least one month of persistent concern about experiencing additional attacks, worry about the consequences of attacks, or a significant maladaptive change in behavior related to the attacks (such as avoiding unfamiliar locations or physical exercise). A panic attack is an abrupt surge of intense fear or intense discomfort that reaches a peak within minutes, during which four or more somatic and cognitive symptoms occur.\n\nA foundational diagnostic distinction in DSM-5 is that a Panic Attack is not a mental disorder in itself. Instead, the 'Panic Attack Specifier' is a transdiagnostic specifier that can be applied to ANY disorder in the DSM-5 (e.g., Major Depressive Disorder with panic attacks; PTSD with panic attacks; Social Anxiety Disorder with panic attacks), as well as general medical conditions.\n\nTo diagnose Panic Disorder, panic attacks must be unexpected—meaning there is no obvious cue or trigger at the time of occurrence ('out of the blue'). Attacks that are consistently triggered by a specific phobic stimulus (e.g., seeing a snake or public speaking) represent expected panic attacks and are diagnosed under the relevant phobic disorder rather than Panic Disorder.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Criterion A requires recurrent unexpected panic attacks. A panic attack is an abrupt surge of intense fear or discomfort that reaches a peak within minutes, with four (or more) of the following 13 symptoms:\n1. Palpitations, pounding heart, or accelerated heart rate.\n2. Sweating.\n3. Trembling or shaking.\n4. Sensations of shortness of breath or smothering.\n5. Feelings of choking.\n6. Chest pain or discomfort.\n7. Nausea or abdominal distress.\n8. Feeling dizzy, unsteady, light-headed, or faint.\n9. Chills or heat sensations.\n10. Paresthesias (numbness or tingling sensations).\n11. Derealization (feelings of unreality) or depersonalization (being detached from oneself).\n12. Fear of losing control or 'going crazy'.\n13. Fear of dying.\n\nCriterion B mandates that at least one of the attacks has been followed by 1 month (or more) of one or both of the following:\n1. Persistent concern or worry about additional panic attacks or their consequences (e.g., losing control, having a heart attack, 'going crazy').\n2. A significant maladaptive change in behavior related to the attacks (e.g., behaviors designed to avoid having panic attacks, such as avoidance of exercise or unfamiliar situations).\n\nCriterion C requires ruling out substances and medical conditions (e.g., hyperthyroidism, pheochromocytoma, cardiopulmonary conditions). Criterion D dictates that the disturbance is not better explained by another mental disorder.",
        'specifiers' => "The Panic Attack Specifier is coded across DSM-5 disorders: e.g., 'Generalized Anxiety Disorder, with panic attacks'. Within Panic Disorder itself, clinicians specify the presence or absence of concurrent Agoraphobia (which is coded as a separate comorbid diagnosis under DSM-5).",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Panic Disorder vs. Medical Conditions: Acute myocardial infarction, pheochromocytoma, supraventricular tachycardia (SVT), and hyperthyroidism must be excluded through ECG, cardiac enzymes, and thyroid panels.\n\nPanic Disorder vs. Agoraphobia: In DSM-5, Panic Disorder and Agoraphobia are two separate, independent diagnoses. A patient can have Panic Disorder alone, Agoraphobia alone, or both co-diagnosed.\n\nPanic Disorder vs. Specific Phobia / Social Anxiety: If panic attacks occur ONLY in response to specific phobic triggers (e.g., heights, spiders, public speaking), Specific Phobia or Social Anxiety Disorder is diagnosed, NOT Panic Disorder.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 28-year-old teacher who experienced a sudden, unprovoked episode while relaxing at home. Within five minutes, the teacher experienced severe chest tightness, heart palpitations, shortness of breath, trembling, tingling in fingers (paresthesias), and an overwhelming terror of dying.\n\nEmergency department medical and cardiac workups are completely normal. Over the subsequent six weeks, the teacher has experienced three additional unprovoked attacks and has developed constant dread of future attacks, refusing to drive or exercise for fear of triggering heart palpitations. The presence of recurrent unexpected attacks with >1 month of anticipatory worry and behavioral avoidance confirms Panic Disorder.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: Panic attacks reach peak intensity within minutes and require at least 4 of 13 symptoms. Panic Disorder requires recurrent UNEXPECTED attacks followed by at least 1 month of anticipatory worry or maladaptive behavioral change. In DSM-5, Panic Disorder and Agoraphobia are two separate, independent diagnoses that can be co-diagnosed. The Panic Attack Specifier is transdiagnostic and can be applied to any DSM-5 disorder. Medical mimics include pheochromocytoma and hyperthyroidism.",
