    3 => [
        'title' => 'Agoraphobia: Fear of 2+ Public/Enclosed/Open Spaces',
        'slug' => 'agoraphobia-diagnostic-criteria',
        'description' => 'The 2 of 5 situational domains rule, escape/help concerns, 6-month duration, and independent diagnostic separation from Panic Disorder.',
        'overview' => "Agoraphobia represents a distinct, disabling anxiety disorder characterized by marked fear or anxiety about being in situations where escape might be difficult or help might not be available in the event of developing panic-like symptoms or other incapacitating or embarrassing symptoms (e.g., fear of falling in the elderly, fear of incontinence). Rather than representing an isolated fear of open spaces, agoraphobia is fundamentally rooted in fear of trapped vulnerability.\n\nA major structural change in DSM-5 was the complete uncoupling of Agoraphobia from Panic Disorder. In DSM-IV, agoraphobia was largely classified as a complication of panic disorder ('Panic Disorder with Agoraphobia'). DSM-5 established Agoraphobia as an independent, standalone diagnostic entity that can be diagnosed regardless of the presence or absence of Panic Disorder. Epidemiological data confirms that a substantial proportion of individuals with agoraphobia develop fear and avoidance without ever experiencing full panic attacks.\n\nTo establish the diagnosis under DSM-5, fear or anxiety must be documented across at least two of five discrete situational domains. Furthermore, the situations almost always provoke fear, are actively avoided or endured with intense dread, and the disturbance must persist for at least six months.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Criterion A requires marked fear or anxiety about two (or more) of the following five situations:\n1. Using public transportation (e.g., automobiles, buses, trains, ships, planes).\n2. Being in open spaces (e.g., parking lots, marketplaces, bridges).\n3. Being in enclosed places (e.g., shops, theaters, cinemas).\n4. Standing in line or being in a crowd.\n5. Being outside of the home alone.\n\nCriterion B mandates that the individual fears or avoids these situations because of thoughts that escape might be difficult or help might not be available in the event of developing panic-like symptoms or other incapacitating or embarrassing symptoms (e.g., fear of falling in elderly; fear of incontinence).\n\nCriterion C establishes that the agoraphobic situations almost always provoke fear or anxiety.\n\nCriterion D requires that the situations are actively avoided, require the presence of a companion, or are endured with intense fear or anxiety.\n\nCriterion E requires that the fear or anxiety is out of proportion to the actual danger posed by the agoraphobic situations and to the sociocultural context.\n\nCriterion F establishes the duration threshold: The fear, anxiety, or avoidance is persistent, typically lasting for 6 months or more.\n\nCriterion G requires clinically significant distress or functional impairment. Criterion H and I require ruling out medical conditions and verifying symptoms are not better explained by another mental disorder.",
        'specifiers' => "Agoraphobia is coded as a standalone disorder. If an individual meets criteria for both Panic Disorder and Agoraphobia, BOTH diagnoses are assigned concurrently.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Agoraphobia vs. Specific Phobia (Situational Type): If fear and avoidance are restricted to only ONE situational category (e.g., fear of airplanes only, or fear of elevators only), the diagnosis is Specific Phobia, NOT Agoraphobia. Agoraphobia requires fear across at least TWO distinct domains.\n\nAgoraphobia vs. Social Anxiety Disorder: In Social Anxiety Disorder, avoidance is driven by fear of being negatively evaluated, scrutinized, or judged; in Agoraphobia, avoidance is driven by fear of being unable to escape or find help during panic/incapacitation.\n\nAgoraphobia vs. Major Depressive Disorder: In MDD, individuals may remain at home due to apathy, anhedonia, and fatigue; in Agoraphobia, they remain at home due to fear of panic/incapacitation in public.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 40-year-old woman who has avoided grocery stores (enclosed spaces) and public city buses (public transportation) for the past eight months. She reports intense anxiety that if she enters these places, she will become severely dizzy, faint, and be unable to get to safety.\n\nShe requires her spouse to accompany her whenever she leaves the house. She has never had a full panic attack. Meeting criteria across two distinct domains (enclosed spaces and public transportation) with fear of incapacitation persisting for >6 months and requiring a companion, the clinical formulation confirms Agoraphobia as a standalone diagnosis.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: Agoraphobia requires fear/anxiety in at least 2 of 5 specific situations (public transit, open spaces, enclosed places, lines/crowds, outside home alone). Duration must be >= 6 months. Fear is driven by thoughts that escape might be difficult or help unavailable. In DSM-5, Agoraphobia is an independent diagnosis, separate from Panic Disorder; if both are present, both are coded.",
        'key_concepts' => [
