    5 => [
        'title' => 'Social Anxiety Disorder (Social Phobia & Performance Specifier)',
        'slug' => 'social-anxiety-disorder-performance-specifier',
        'description' => 'Fear of negative evaluation, 6-month persistence, peer-interaction requirements in children, and the performance-only specifier.',
        'overview' => "Social Anxiety Disorder (Social Phobia) is characterized by marked, persistent fear or anxiety about one or more social situations in which the individual is exposed to possible scrutiny by others. Examples include social interactions (having a conversation, meeting unfamiliar people), being observed (eating or drinking in public), and performing in front of others (giving a speech). The underlying cognitive core is an intense, pervasive fear of negative evaluation—that they will act in a way or show anxiety symptoms (blushing, trembling, sweating) that will be negatively evaluated (humiliating, embarrassing, leading to rejection, or offending others).\n\nIn children, the diagnosis requires that social anxiety must occur in peer settings and not just during interactions with adults, ensuring that normal pediatric wariness of adult strangers is not pathologized. Furthermore, children may express social anxiety through crying, tantrums, freezing, clinging, or failing to speak in social situations.\n\nDSM-5 includes a specific clinical specifier: 'Performance only'. This specifier applies when the fear is restricted exclusively to speaking or performing in public. Individuals with performance-only social anxiety have normal social ease in informal interpersonal conversations, but experience disabling autonomic panic when required to perform in front of an audience (e.g., musicians, public speakers, athletes).",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Criterion A requires marked fear or anxiety about one or more social situations in which the individual is exposed to possible scrutiny by others. In children, the anxiety must occur in peer settings and not just during interactions with adults.\n\nCriterion B mandates that the individual fears that he or she will act in a way or show anxiety symptoms that will be negatively evaluated (i.e., will be humiliating or embarrassing; will lead to rejection or offend others).\n\nCriterion C establishes that the social situations almost always provoke fear or anxiety (in children: crying, tantrums, freezing, clinging, shrinking).\n\nCriterion D requires that the social situations are avoided or endured with intense fear or anxiety.\n\nCriterion E mandates that the fear or anxiety is out of proportion to the actual threat posed by the social situation and to the sociocultural context.\n\nCriterion F establishes the duration threshold: The fear, anxiety, or avoidance is persistent, typically lasting for 6 months or more.\n\nCriterion G requires clinically significant distress or functional impairment. Criterion H, I, and J require ruling out substances, medical conditions (e.g., Parkinsonian tremor, severe burns), and other mental disorders.",
        'specifiers' => "Clinicians specify: 'Performance only' if the fear is restricted to speaking or performing in public. (Note: Individuals with performance-only social anxiety typically respond well to beta-blockers such as propranolol taken prior to performance).",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Social Anxiety Disorder vs. Panic Disorder / Agoraphobia: In Social Anxiety, avoidance is driven by fear of negative evaluation/scrutiny; in Agoraphobia, avoidance is driven by fear of trapped incapacitation or inability to escape.\n\nSocial Anxiety Disorder vs. Avoidant Personality Disorder: Substantial clinical overlap exists; Avoidant Personality Disorder represents a more pervasive, severe, lifelong pattern of feelings of inadequacy and hypersensitivity across all spheres. Many clinicians view them as overlapping points on a severity continuum.\n\nSocial Anxiety Disorder vs. Selective Mutism: Children with Selective Mutism consistently fail to speak in specific social situations despite speaking normally at home, but do not necessarily fear negative scrutiny.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 26-year-old software developer who experiences extreme dread regarding team meetings and lunchtime socializing. The developer avoids eating in the cafeteria out of fear that coworkers will notice their hands trembling, avoids asking questions in meetings due to terror of sounding foolish, and has declined promotions to avoid leading group discussions.\n\nThis pattern has persisted for over two years, causing marked career stagnation. Meeting criteria across social interactions and being observed, persisting >6 months, driven by fear of negative evaluation, the clinical formulation confirms Social Anxiety Disorder (Generalized, not performance only).",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: Social Anxiety Disorder requires fear of negative evaluation in social scrutiny situations lasting >= 6 months. In children, social anxiety must occur in peer settings, not just with adults. The 'Performance only' specifier applies when fear is limited to public speaking or performing. Beta-blockers (e.g., propranolol) are effective for performance-only social anxiety. Differentiate from Agoraphobia (fear of trapped incapacitation) and Avoidant Personality Disorder.",
