        'slug' => 'separation-anxiety-selective-mutism',
        'description' => 'Separation anxiety in adults (>= 6 months) vs. children (>= 4 weeks), attachment dynamics, and Selective Mutism (1-month school rule).',
        'overview' => "Separation Anxiety Disorder and Selective Mutism are neurodevelopmentally linked anxiety disorders that share core origins in early childhood attachment and social communication, though both carry significant clinical manifestations across the lifespan.\n\nIn DSM-5, a major structural update was the removal of Separation Anxiety Disorder from the historical 'Disorders Usually First Diagnosed in Infancy, Childhood, or Adolescence' section and its relocation to the core Anxiety Disorders chapter. Crucially, DSM-5 recognized that Separation Anxiety Disorder can have its onset in adulthood and commonly persists across mature individuals, manifesting as excessive fear or anxiety concerning separation from attachment figures (spouses, children, romantic partners). Duration requirements reflect developmental stage: symptoms must persist for at least 4 weeks in children/adolescents, and at least 6 months in adults.\n\nSelective Mutism is characterized by a consistent failure to speak in specific social situations where there is an expectation to speak (e.g., in school) despite speaking fluently in other situations (e.g., at home with parents). The disorder must persist for at least one month, and crucially, DSM-5 establishes that this month cannot be limited to the first month of school, recognizing that transient shyness is normal during initial classroom transitions.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "For Separation Anxiety Disorder: Criterion A requires developmentally inappropriate and excessive fear or anxiety concerning separation from those to whom the individual is attached, evidenced by at least three of the following:\n1. Recurrent excessive distress when anticipating or experiencing separation from home or attachment figures.\n2. Persistent worry about losing major attachment figures or about possible harm to them (illness, death).\n3. Persistent worry about experiencing an untoward event (getting lost, kidnapped) that causes separation.\n4. Persistent reluctance or refusal to go out, away from home, to school, work, or elsewhere because of fear of separation.\n5. Persistent fear or reluctance about being alone or without attachment figures.\n6. Persistent reluctance or refusal to sleep away from home or to go to sleep without being near attachment figure.\n7. Repeated nightmares involving the theme of separation.\n8. Repeated complaints of physical symptoms (headaches, stomachaches, nausea) when separation occurs or is anticipated.\nDuration: At least 4 weeks in children/adolescents; at least 6 months in adults.\n\nFor Selective Mutism: Criterion A requires consistent failure to speak in specific social situations where there is an expectation to speak (e.g., school) despite speaking in other situations. Criterion B mandates that the disturbance interferes with educational/occupational achievement. Criterion C establishes that the duration is at least 1 month (not limited to the first month of school). Criterion D requires that failure to speak is not due to lack of knowledge of spoken language. Criterion E requires symptoms are not better explained by a communication disorder or ASD.",
        'specifiers' => "Selective Mutism and Separation Anxiety Disorder are coded independently, noting clinical specifiers and comorbid social anxiety.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Selective Mutism vs. Social (Pragmatic) Communication Disorder: In SPCD, language pragmatic abilities are impaired across ALL settings; in Selective Mutism, linguistic and pragmatic competence is completely normal in comfortable settings (at home).\n\nSeparation Anxiety in Adults vs. Agoraphobia: In Agoraphobia, avoidance of leaving home is driven by fear of trapped panic or incapacitation; in Separation Anxiety, it is driven by terror of physical separation from attachment figures.\n\nSeparation Anxiety vs. Normal Stranger/Separation Anxiety: Normal infant stranger anxiety peaks at 8-10 months and separation protest peaks at 12-18 months; Separation Anxiety Disorder is developmentally inappropriate and causes marked impairment.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 6-year-old child who speaks fluently, reads aloud, and engages in spirited conversations at home with parents and siblings. However, since entering first grade three months ago, the child has not uttered a single word to teachers or classmates, communicating only through nodding or pointing.\n\nAudiological and speech language assessments confirm normal linguistic comprehension. The child has had normal speech at home, and the mutism has persisted for three months (well past the first month of school). The clinical formulation confirms Selective Mutism, with referral for behavioral exposure and school-based behavioral fading.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: Separation Anxiety Disorder can be diagnosed in adults (duration threshold: >= 6 months in adults; >= 4 weeks in children). Selective Mutism requires consistent failure to speak in specific situations for at least 1 month, NOT limited to the first month of school. The child speaks fluently in other settings (e.g., at home). Selective Mutism is strongly comorbid with Social Anxiety Disorder. Linguistic competence must be intact in the native language.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Separation Anxiety Across Lifespan', 'icon' => '🌱', 'definition' => 'DSM-5 formally recognized adult-onset separation anxiety, setting a 6-month threshold for adults and 4 weeks for children.'],
            ['title' => 'The First Month of School Rule', 'icon' => '🏫', 'definition' => 'Selective Mutism requires at least 1 month of failure to speak, which cannot be restricted to the initial transition month of school.'],
            ['title' => 'Situational Speech Specificity', 'icon' => '🤐', 'definition' => 'The individual speaks normally in familiar domestic settings but consistently fails to speak where speech is expected.'],
            ['title' => 'Attachment Figure Focus', 'icon' => '🫂', 'definition' => 'Distress and avoidance are anchored to physical separation from primary attachment figures (parents, spouses, children).']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Attachment Figure', 'definition' => 'A primary person (parent, spouse, caregiver) with whom an individual has formed a strong emotional bond that provides safety and security.'],
            ['term' => 'Stimulus Fading', 'definition' => 'A behavioral therapy technique used in Selective Mutism where a familiar person is gradually transitioned out while new communication partners are introduced.'],
            ['term' => 'School Refusal', 'definition' => 'A behavioral presentation where a child refuses to attend school, frequently driven by Separation Anxiety Disorder rather than truancy.'],
            ['term' => 'Behavioral Inhibition', 'definition' => 'A temperamental trait characterized by extreme distress, withdrawal, and avoidance in the face of unfamiliar people, objects, or settings.']
        ]
    ],
    7 => [
        'title' => 'Biological Pathways, Amygdala Hyperactivity, and Course',
        'slug' => 'biological-pathways-amygdala-hyperactivity-course',
        'description' => 'The central fear network (amygdala, hippocampus, vmPFC), GABA-A and serotonin neurochemistry, and chronic anxiety course.',
        'overview' => "The neurobiology of Anxiety Disorders is anchored in the dysregulation of the central fear and threat-processing network, comprising the basolateral amygdala, the central nucleus of the amygdala, the hippocampus, the anterior cingulate cortex (ACC), and the ventromedial prefrontal cortex (vmPFC). In healthy individuals, the vmPFC provides inhibitory, top-down cognitive modulation over the amygdala, dampening fear responses once a perceived threat is recognized as benign.\n\nIn pathological anxiety, this regulatory equilibrium is severely disrupted: functional neuroimaging consistently demonstrates amygdala hyperreactivity paired with hypoactive prefrontal cortical control. The central nucleus of the amygdala projects directly to effector regions: to the lateral hypothalamus (triggering sympathetic autonomic activation, tachycardia, and hypertension), to the locus coeruleus (releasing norepinephrine and enhancing vigilance), and to the periaqueductal gray (mediating freezing behavior).\n\nAt the neurochemical level, anxiety disorders involve deficits in GABA-ergic inhibitory neurotransmission (leading to unconstrained neuronal excitability) alongside dysregulation in central serotonergic (5-HT) and noradrenergic circuits. Furthermore, the hippocampus plays a pivotal role in contextual fear conditioning; under chronic stress, impaired hippocampal neurogenesis compromises the brain's ability to distinguish safe contexts from dangerous contexts, resulting in generalized fear overextension.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "While neurobiological pathways do not represent distinct DSM-5 diagnostic categories, evaluating these mechanisms is essential for assigning appropriate somatic treatments and understanding the longitudinal course of anxiety disorders.\n\nClinicians must evaluate: (1) autonomic sympathetic reactivity (palpitations, tremors, sweating); (2) neurocognitive hypervigilance and attentional bias toward threat; (3) behavioral avoidance patterns that prevent fear extinction; and (4) family genetic loading across the anxiety spectrum.",
        'specifiers' => "Dimensional severity rating scales and documentation of comorbid depressive or substance use disorders.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Evaluating biological anxiety against medical mimics: Pheochromocytoma (excessive catecholamines), Hyperthyroidism (elevated free T4), Mitral Valve Prolapse with dysautonomia, Temporal Lobe Seizures, and Paroxysmal Atrial Tachycardia.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 22-year-old patient who developed panic attacks and agoraphobic avoidance following a motor vehicle collision. Functional neuroimaging and physiological monitoring reveal intense autonomic surges to minor road sounds, accompanied by exaggerated amygdala activation and failure of vmPFC extinction recall.\n\nThe clinical formulation identifies failure of fear extinction circuitry, providing the rationale for combining selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (to enhance frontolimbic plasticity) with Cognitive Behavioral Therapy featuring Interoceptive and In Vivo Exposure (to restore prefrontal inhibitory control over the amygdala).",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: The amygdala is the central hub of fear processing, projecting to the hypothalamus (autonomic surge) and locus coeruleus (norepinephrine). The ventromedial prefrontal cortex (vmPFC) provides top-down inhibitory control; this inhibition is deficient in anxiety disorders. GABA-A receptor positive modulators (benzodiazepines) provide acute symptom relief by enhancing chloride influx, while SSRIs provide long-term neuroplastic remodeling. Avoidance maintains anxiety by preventing natural fear extinction.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'The Central Fear Circuit', 'icon' => '🧠', 'definition' => 'The amygdala mediates threat detection, projecting to the hypothalamus (autonomic) and locus coeruleus (norepinephrine).'],
            ['title' => 'Top-Down Prefrontal Failure', 'icon' => '🛑', 'definition' => 'Deficient ventromedial prefrontal cortical (vmPFC) inhibition allows unconstrained amygdala hyperactivity.'],
            ['title' => 'GABA-A Receptor Inhibition', 'icon' => '🧪', 'definition' => 'GABA-ergic neurotransmission enhances chloride influx to hyperpolarize neurons and terminate fear signaling.'],
            ['title' => 'Failure of Fear Extinction', 'icon' => '⛓️', 'definition' => 'Behavioral avoidance prevents the prefrontal cortex from learning that phobic stimuli no longer pose danger.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Locus Coeruleus', 'definition' => 'The principal noradrenergic nucleus in the pons that projects throughout the brain, driving physiological arousal and vigilance in anxiety.'],
            ['term' => 'Periaqueductal Gray (PAG)', 'definition' => 'A midbrain structure that coordinates defensive behaviors, including freezing and fight-or-flight motor responses.'],
            ['term' => 'Fear Extinction', 'definition' => 'A learning process wherein repeated exposure to a conditioned stimulus without the unconditioned aversive event results in diminished fear response.'],
            ['term' => 'Interoceptive Exposure', 'definition' => 'A therapeutic technique wherein patients deliberately induce feared bodily sensations (hyperventilation, spinning) to extinguish panic fear.']
        ]
    ],
    8 => [
        'title' => 'Differential Diagnosis: Panic vs. Medical/Thyroid vs. Social Phobia vs. GAD',
        'slug' => 'differential-diagnosis-anxiety-disorders-medical-mimics',
        'description' => 'Systematic differential algorithm: medical rule-outs (pheochromocytoma, hyperthyroidism), focus of apprehension, and duration rules.',
        'overview' => "The differential diagnosis of Anxiety Disorders requires navigating complex overlaps between medical conditions, somatic symptoms, and discrete anxiety syndromes. Because the autonomic sympathetic nervous system produces identical physical signs—tachycardia, diaphoresis, tremors, shortness of breath—regardless of whether triggered by an adrenaline-secreting tumor, thyrotoxicosis, or panic disorder, clinical acumen hinges on systematic differential algorithms.\n\nThe initial diagnostic imperative demands ruling out somatic and pharmacological etiologies. Once organic causes are excluded, the clinician must determine the specific cognitive 'focus of apprehension'. If worry is focused on multiple everyday life domains -> Generalized Anxiety Disorder. If fear is triggered by sudden unexpected physical surges -> Panic Disorder. If fear is focused on negative social scrutiny -> Social Anxiety Disorder. If fear is focused on trapped incapacitation across public spaces -> Agoraphobia.\n\nThis module outlines the systematic clinical decision tree used to resolve overlapping anxiety presentations, ensuring accurate diagnosis and preventing inappropriate pharmacotherapy.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Systematic Differential Algorithm:\n\n1. Step 1: Medical & Toxicological Rule-Out. Verify absence of hyperthyroidism (TSH/free T4), pheochromocytoma (urine metanephrines), cardiac arrhythmias (ECG/Holter), pulmonary embolism, caffeine toxicity, or stimulant use.\n\n2. Step 2: Determine Focus of Apprehension:\n   • Focus is on negative social evaluation/scrutiny -> Social Anxiety Disorder.\n   • Focus is on unexpected panic attacks and bodily catastrophe -> Panic Disorder.\n   • Focus is on inability to escape or find help in 2+ public/open/enclosed situations -> Agoraphobia.\n   • Focus is on diffuse, multi-domain everyday life events -> Generalized Anxiety Disorder.\n   • Focus is on an isolated object or animal -> Specific Phobia.\n   • Focus is on separation from an attachment figure -> Separation Anxiety Disorder.\n\n3. Step 3: Check Duration Parameters: GAD, Agoraphobia, Social Anxiety, and Specific Phobia all require >= 6 months. Separation Anxiety requires >= 4 weeks in children / >= 6 months in adults. Panic Disorder requires >= 1 month of anticipatory worry/behavioral change.",
        'specifiers' => "Diagnostic formulations specify primary diagnoses, secondary comorbid anxiety/depressive disorders, and transdiagnostic panic attack specifiers.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Hypoglycemia vs. Panic Attack: Reactive hypoglycemia produces acute sweating, tremor, and palpitations responsive to glucose administration.\n\nPheochromocytoma vs. Panic Disorder: Pheochromocytoma features the classic triad of episodic headaches, sweating, and severe paroxysmal hypertension, typically lacking the psychological agoraphobic avoidance of panic disorder.\n\nAsthma vs. Panic Disorder: Pulmonary function testing and bronchodilator response distinguish bronchospasm from hyperventilation-induced panic.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 29-year-old accountant presenting with paroxysmal episodes of tachycardia, diaphoresis, and shaking. Medical evaluation reveals elevated blood pressure and a 24-hour urine collection with markedly elevated fractionated metanephrines and catecholamines.\n\nAbdominal MRI identifies a 3 cm adrenal medullary mass. The episodes represent secondary autonomic surges driven by an adrenaline-secreting tumor rather than a primary psychiatric anxiety condition. The correct diagnosis is Anxiety Disorder Due to Another Medical Condition (Pheochromocytoma), directing the patient to endocrine surgery.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: The critical differentiator among anxiety disorders is the focus of apprehension: GAD = diffuse life worries; Social Anxiety = scrutiny/embarrassment; Panic = unexpected attacks/bodily catastrophe; Agoraphobia = trapped/no help. Medical mimics: Pheochromocytoma (paroxysmal hypertension, headache, sweating, palpitations; diagnose with urine metanephrines); Hyperthyroidism (weight loss, heat intolerance; check TSH). Panic disorder requires 1 month of worry; other primary anxiety disorders require 6 months.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Focus of Apprehension Principle', 'icon' => '🎯', 'definition' => 'Differentiating anxiety disorders by identifying the exact cognitive content of what the patient dreads.'],
            ['title' => 'Medical Mimicry Triad', 'icon' => '🔬', 'definition' => 'Ruling out pheochromocytoma, hyperthyroidism, and cardiac dysrhythmias prior to assigning primary anxiety disorders.'],
            ['title' => 'Duration Parameter Matrix', 'icon' => '⏱️', 'definition' => 'Commanding duration thresholds: 1 month for Panic Disorder; 6 months for GAD, Agoraphobia, Social Anxiety, Phobia.'],
            ['title' => 'Sympathetic vs. Endocrine Differentiation', 'icon' => '🧪', 'definition' => 'Distinguishing autonomic fear surges from systemic metabolic and hormonal hyperadrenergic states.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Focus of Apprehension', 'definition' => 'The specific core threat, event, or consequence that forms the primary target of an individual\'s anxiety and avoidance.'],
            ['term' => 'Metanephrines', 'definition' => 'Metabolites of epinephrine and norepinephrine measured in urine or plasma to diagnose pheochromocytoma.'],
            ['term' => 'Hyperthyroidism', 'definition' => 'Overproduction of thyroid hormone producing a hypermetabolic state with tremor, palpitations, anxiety, and weight loss.'],
            ['term' => 'Mitral Valve Prolapse', 'definition' => 'A cardiac valvular condition historically associated with palpitations, dysautonomia, and panic-like anxiety presentations.']
        ]
    ],
    9 => [
        'title' => 'Educational Case Scenarios: Diagnostic Differential Analysis',
        'slug' => 'educational-case-scenarios-anxiety-disorders',
        'description' => 'Clinical case vignettes, multi-setting evaluation, differential trees, and licensure board question dissection.',
        'overview' => "Clinical competency in anxiety psychopathology requires mastering differential analysis across complex clinical vignettes. Because patients rarely present with textbook, isolated symptoms—frequently exhibiting overlapping somatic complaints, avoidance behaviors, and demoralization—the clinician must systematically dissect presenting symptoms to isolate primary diagnoses from secondary reactions.\n\nThis educational module analyzes multi-layered clinical cases designed to simulate board examination questions and complex outpatient consultations. By walking through real-world clinical decision trees, students and clinicians learn to identify subtle diagnostic clues, recognize medical mimics, apply DSM-5 duration rules, and establish defensible clinical formulations.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Case Formulation Methodology:\n1. Step 1: Medical/Toxicological Screen. Are physical signs secondary to substances, medications, or somatic disease? If NO -> Proceed to psychiatric analysis.\n2. Step 2: Identify Core Triggers. Are attacks unexpected (Panic Disorder) or triggered by social scrutiny (Social Anxiety), specific objects (Specific Phobia), or trapped spaces (Agoraphobia)?\n3. Step 3: Verify Chronicity and Duration. Verify the 6-month rule for GAD/Agoraphobia/Phobia/Social Anxiety, or the 1-month rule for Panic Disorder.\n4. Step 4: Evaluate Comorbidity and Assign Specifiers.",
        'specifiers' => "Diagnostic formulation must record primary diagnoses, clinical specifiers (e.g., 'Performance only', 'With panic attacks'), and functional impairment levels.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Case analyses systematically contrast: Panic Disorder vs. Agoraphobia; GAD vs. Social Anxiety; Specific Phobia vs. OCD; and Separation Anxiety vs. Dependent Personality Disorder.",
        'clinical_example' => "Educational Case Vignette: A 31-year-old violinist presents with severe anxiety. In daily life, the violinist is outgoing, enjoys socializing at crowded dinner parties, and travels internationally without distress. However, over the past eight months, whenever required to perform a solo on stage, the violinist experiences severe hand tremors, palpitations, and nausea, fearing that the audience will notice the shaking and criticize the performance.\n\nEvaluation reveals that the anxiety is restricted strictly to public musical performance. Daily social interactions and solitary practice are completely anxiety-free. The formulation establishes Social Anxiety Disorder, With Performance Only Specifier, identifying a targeted indication for pre-performance beta-blocker therapy (propranolol) alongside cognitive restructuring.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: Systematic case analysis requires identifying the specific trigger and cognitive fear. Isolated performance anxiety in otherwise socially confident individuals warrants Social Anxiety Disorder, Performance only. Always check duration milestones (6 months for social anxiety). Avoidance of multiple public spaces due to fear of trapped panic confirms Agoraphobia. Panic Disorder requires unexpected attacks.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Clinical Case Dissection Trees', 'icon' => '🌳', 'definition' => 'Step-by-step algorithms used to deconstruct complex multi-symptom clinical presentations into discrete DSM-5 entities.'],
            ['title' => 'Trigger Specificity Analysis', 'icon' => '🎯', 'definition' => 'Pinpointing whether anxiety is cued by performance, social evaluation, physical entrapment, or unprovoked surges.'],
            ['title' => 'Chronicity Verification', 'icon' => '⏱️', 'definition' => 'Validating that symptom persistence satisfies the established 1-month or 6-month diagnostic boundaries.'],
            ['title' => 'Functional Context Triangulation', 'icon' => '📐', 'definition' => 'Assessing how anxiety impacts occupational, academic, and domestic spheres of everyday functioning.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Performance Anxiety', 'definition' => 'A specific manifestation of social anxiety restricted exclusively to speaking or performing before an audience.'],
            ['term' => 'Cued vs. Uncued Attack', 'definition' => 'Cued attacks are triggered by an obvious phobic stimulus; uncued attacks occur unexpectedly without an environmental trigger.'],
            ['term' => 'Differential Dissection', 'definition' => 'The analytical clinical method of systematically evaluating each competing diagnosis against clinical evidence to confirm the correct entity.'],
            ['term' => 'Avoidance Hierarchy', 'definition' => 'A graded list of feared situations ranked from least to most distressing, utilized in exposure therapy for anxiety disorders.']
        ]
    ],
    10 => [
        'title' => 'Chapter Mastery & Board-Exam Review',
        'slug' => 'chapter-mastery-board-exam-review-anxiety',
        'description' => 'Comprehensive synthesis of Anxiety Disorders, duration thresholds, diagnostic hierarchies, and high-yield board pearls.',
        'overview' => "This capstone unit delivers an advanced synthesis of the Anxiety Disorders chapter under DSM-5 criteria, consolidating core diagnostic formulations, duration thresholds, and high-yield board pearls for professional licensure examinations. Anxiety disorders represent fundamental, universally tested topics across clinical examinations, demanding exact command over diagnostic criteria, specifiers, and differential boundaries.\n\nMastery requires commanding the clinical distinctions separating Generalized Anxiety Disorder, Panic Disorder, Agoraphobia, Specific Phobia, Social Anxiety Disorder, Separation Anxiety Disorder, and Selective Mutism. Furthermore, candidates must navigate DSM-5 structural refinements, including the independent separation of Agoraphobia from Panic Disorder, the transdiagnostic Panic Attack Specifier, the recognition of adult Separation Anxiety Disorder, and the 1-month Selective Mutism rule.\n\nBy synthesizing clinical vignettes, diagnostic decision matrices, and risk assessment strategies, this unit prepares students and clinicians for board examination excellence and compassionate clinical practice.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Mastery Summary of Core Anxiety Diagnostic Rules:\n\n1. Generalized Anxiety Disorder: Excessive worry >= 6 months across multiple domains; 3 of 6 physical symptoms in adults (1 in children); muscle tension is most specific symptom.\n\n2. Panic Disorder: Recurrent UNEXPECTED panic attacks (4 of 13 symptoms reaching peak within minutes), followed by >= 1 month of anticipatory worry or maladaptive behavioral change.\n\n3. Agoraphobia: Fear/anxiety in >= 2 of 5 situations (public transit, open spaces, enclosed places, lines/crowds, outside home alone); >= 6 months; fear of trapped incapacitation. Independent of Panic Disorder.\n\n4. Specific Phobia: Fear of specific object/situation for >= 6 months; out of proportion; 5 subtypes (Animal, Natural Environment, Blood-Injection-Injury, Situational, Other). BII causes vasovagal syncope.\n\n5. Social Anxiety Disorder: Fear of negative evaluation in social scrutiny situations for >= 6 months; peer settings required in children; 'Performance only' specifier.\n\n6. Separation Anxiety: Inappropriate fear of separation; >= 4 weeks in children / >= 6 months in adults.\n\n7. Selective Mutism: Failure to speak in specific situations despite speaking at home; >= 1 month (not limited to first month of school).",
        'specifiers' => "Review of essential specifiers: Transdiagnostic 'With panic attacks' specifier; 'Performance only' in Social Anxiety Disorder; and Phobic subtypes in Specific Phobia.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Key Board Differential Pearls:\n• Worry about everything for 8 months with muscle tension -> GAD.\n• Sudden out-of-the-blue racing heart, chest pain, fear of dying -> Panic Attack; recurrent with 1 month worry -> Panic Disorder.\n• Avoids elevators, flying, and crowded malls fearing no escape -> Agoraphobia (2+ domains).\n• Avoids elevators only, fine in crowds and flying -> Specific Phobia, Situational type (only 1 domain).\n• Faints when seeing blood -> Specific Phobia, Blood-injection-injury type (vasovagal syncope).\n• Terrified of speaking in public but fine at parties -> Social Anxiety Disorder, Performance only.\n• Child speaks at home but refuses to speak in classroom for 3 months -> Selective Mutism.",
        'clinical_example' => "A comprehensive clinical review scenario: A board examination vignette describes a 32-year-old engineer who reports that over the past seven months, they have avoided subway trains and movie theaters. The engineer explains that if they are in a crowded theater or train and have a sudden medical emergency, they would be trapped and unable to get out to an emergency room.\n\nBoard Exam Question Formulation: What is the diagnosis? The candidate recognizes that fear across two distinct domains (public transit and enclosed spaces) driven by thoughts that escape might be difficult, persisting for >6 months, confirms Agoraphobia as an independent diagnosis under DSM-5.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield board pearls summary: GAD = 6 months, 3 of 6 symptoms (1 in children). Panic Disorder = unexpected attacks + 1 month worry. Agoraphobia = 2 of 5 situations for 6 months (independent diagnosis). BII phobia = vasovagal syncope treated with applied tension. Social Anxiety = fear of negative evaluation for 6 months (performance-only specifier). Selective Mutism = 1 month not limited to first month of school. Separation anxiety occurs in adults (6 months).",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Anxiety Diagnostic Decision Matrix', 'icon' => '🏛️', 'definition' => 'Comprehensive command of duration thresholds, symptom counts, and severity rules across all anxiety spectrum disorders.'],
            ['title' => 'The Agoraphobia Independence Rule', 'icon' => '🚪', 'definition' => 'Commanding the DSM-5 structural separation of Agoraphobia from Panic Disorder as independent codable entities.'],
            ['title' => 'The Blood-Injection-Injury Exception', 'icon' => '🩸', 'definition' => 'Recognizing parasympathetic bradycardia/hypotension and applying applied tension therapy.'],
            ['title' => 'The Focus of Apprehension Algorithm', 'icon' => '🎯', 'definition' => 'Decisive identification of what the patient fears: scrutiny (social), trapped (agoraphobia), bodily catastrophe (panic), diffuse life (GAD).']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Panic Attack Specifier', 'definition' => 'A transdiagnostic DSM-5 descriptor indicating the occurrence of panic attacks across any psychiatric or medical condition.'],
            ['term' => 'Interoceptive Conditioning', 'definition' => 'Classical conditioning wherein internal physiological cues (elevated heart rate, shortness of breath) become conditioned stimuli triggering acute panic.'],
            ['term' => 'Agoraphobic Avoidance', 'definition' => 'Behavioral constriction wherein an individual refuses to enter environments where exit is constrained or help unavailable.'],
            ['term' => 'Performance Specifier', 'definition' => 'A subtype designation in Social Anxiety Disorder indicating that social anxiety is restricted strictly to public speaking or performance.']
        ]
    ]
];
